# pa_pair.txtar
#
# testscript: executable
#
# `cascade pa pair` — the issuance half of Epic W's bridge pairing flow
# (P1-E23-W5-S48-T1, R-14.71's §D-21 COMMANDS mount, RPC pa.pair_code). Run in
# process by TestScript (script_test.go) against the REAL composition root, so
# what this asserts is what an operator's own shell would see.
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT CAN AND CANNOT PROVE. Issuance is a DAEMON verb: the code is
# minted by the process that verifies it, because the code's stored digest is
# keyed from the bot token (internal/daemon/subsystem_bridge.go,
# internal/plugins/cascadepa_bridge_wiring.go). A script runs no daemon, so what
# it proves is the command surface: that `pa pair` is mounted, that it never
# prompts, that its argument handling refuses more than one subject, and that
# with no daemon listening it says so HONESTLY instead of printing a code nothing
# could verify. The issue-and-redeem round trip is proved where it happens, over
# the real store: internal/plugins TestBridgeIssuance_IsRedeemableThroughTheSame-
# DurableRow, and the daemon subsystem's own tests.
#
# SCOPE DEVIATION, RECORDED: the ticket's files_scope names these scripts at
# plugins/cascade-pa/cmd/testscript/*.txtar. Nothing globs that directory —
# cmd/cascade/script_test.go globs cmd/cascade/testdata/scripts/*.txtar and runs
# only files carrying the header marker above. Written here so they RUN; a check
# spelled against an inert file passes by naming nothing (R-14.268).

# With no daemon on this script's own CASCADE_HOME, the command reports the
# transport honestly and names the verb it could not reach.
! exec cascade pa pair tg-scripttest
stderr 'pa\.pair_code'
stderr 'daemon not running or unreachable'

# It never invents a code to print, and it never prompts.
! stdout 'pairing code'
! stdout '[0-9A-HJKMNP-TV-Z]{8}'
! stdout '[Cc]ontinue\?'

# --json fails the same way: a machine-readable envelope is never fabricated.
! exec cascade pa pair --json tg-scripttest
! stdout '"code"'
stderr 'daemon not running or unreachable'

# Omitting the subject is legal at the command surface — the DAEMON decides which
# bridge it runs — so the refusal is still the transport's, not an argument's.
! exec cascade pa pair
stderr 'daemon not running or unreachable'

# More than one positional argument is refused rather than ignored, and that
# refusal comes from the command itself, before any transport is attempted.
! exec cascade pa pair tg-one tg-two
stderr 'accepts at most 1 arg'
! stderr 'daemon not running'
